==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
Current use as slogan popularized after {{l|en|Holocaust|the Holocaust}}, including by [[w:Meir Kahane|Meir Kahane]]'s titular 1972 book and [[w:Jewish Defense League|Jewish Defense League]]'s 1968 adoption of the slogan.<ref>https://mosaicmagazine.com/observation/history-ideas/2017/06/what-is-the-source-of-the-phrase-never-again/</ref><ref>https://www.timesofisrael.com/how-never-again-evolved-from-holocaust-commemoration-slogan-to-universal-call/</ref>

===Adverb===
{{en-adv|-}}

# {{lb|en|set phrase}} At no time in the future; {{non-gloss|usually said of a negative event, practice, etc., which should not be allowed to [[reoccur]].}}
#* {{quote-text|en|passage='''Never again''' becomes more than a slogan: It’s a prayer, a promise, a vow …  '''never again''' the glorification of base, ugly, dark violence|author=Elie Wiesel|year=2012|url=https://www.jpost.com/diaspora/never-again-from-a-holocaust-phrase-to-a-universal-phrase-544666}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2014|author=Jeffrey D. Wilhelm; Whitney Douglas; Sara W. Fry|title=The Activist Learner: Inquiry, Literacy, and Service to Make Learning Better|publisher=Teachers College Press|isbn=9780807755952|page=93
|passage=Yet, as Rose says, "'''Never again''' is obsolete. '''Never again''' is now.” She points out that there are contemporary genocides and many human rights violations happening at this very second. Rose's words challenge us all to respond to the moral imperative of standing up when confronted by injustice,&nbsp;...}}
#* {{quote-web|1=en|date=2025-01-28|work=w:Council on Foreign Relations|title=The Fall of Goma|url=https://www.cfr.org/blog/fall-goma|text=What could be more cynical than a cash grab wrapped up in “'''never again'''?” }}

